{"genre":"Essay","passages":[{"id":"T1","text":"Social responsibility is a crucial element for thriving organizations, and it’s imperative for them to show that they genuinely interact with their stakeholders and contribute positively to society. The World Bank defines social accountability as measures taken by countries or multinational businesses to ensure respect for laws, cultural and social diversity, and environmental protection. This commitment is key to fulfilling stakeholder expectations and fostering trust.

A prime example is a company aiming to establish an eco-friendly brand. Recognizing the importance of sustainability, this firm undertook thorough research, studying the sustainable practices of other companies. They then developed a groundbreaking product and committed to supporting environmentally friendly social initiatives."},{"id":"T2","text":"Social responsibility is a fundamental part of successful companies, and it is essential for companies to demonstrate that they are actively involved with their shareholders and society as a whole. According to the World Bank, social responsibility is defined as the measures taken by a country or a multi-national company to ensure its shareholders that it respects the law, respects cultural and social diversity and protects the environment. Social responsibility is essential to achieve the values of the shareholders and to build trust. Case: A company that wanted to build an eco-friendly brand. The company wanted to build an eco-friendly brand that would be in line with its company’s ethos of sustainability. It conducted extensive research into the sustainable practices of other companies, developed an innovative product and invested in socially responsible initiatives."},{"id":"T3","text":"Social responsibility is crucial for any organisation aiming for success. It’s important for companies to show they are actively involved with their stakeholders and the wider community. The World Bank defines social accountability as ensuring respect for laws, cultural and social diversity, and environmental protection. This helps build trust and aligns with stakeholder values.

For example, one company sought to establish an eco-friendly image that reflected its commitment to sustainability. To do this, they studied other firms' sustainable practices, created a cutting-edge product, and supported various green initiatives."},{"id":"T4","text":"Social responsibility is a fundamental part of successful organizations, and it is essential for organizations to demonstrate that they are actively engaged with their stakeholders and society as a whole. According to the World Bank, social responsibility is the measures taken by a country or a multinational company to ensure its stakeholders that it respects the law, respects cultural and social diversity, and protects the environment. Social responsibility is essential to achieve stakeholder values and build trust across the board. Case: A company that wanted to build an eco-friendly brand The company wanted to build an eco-friendly brand that would be in line with its company's ethos of sustainability. It did a lot of research, learned about the practices of other companies, developed an innovation, and invested in socially friendly social initiatives."}]}

