{"genre":"Essay","passages":[{"id":"T1","text":"Hyperlipemia is an excess of fatty substances in the blood, such as triglycerides and cholesterin. High triglyceride and cholesterin are a risk factor for heart disease, including heart attacks and angina pectoris. Several classes of drugs are used to lower triglyceride and cholesterin and reduce the risk of heart disease. Statins are the most commonly prescribed class of drugs for lowering cholesterin. Statins inhibit the HMG-CoA reductase, an important enzyme in the synthesis of cholesterin. Statins have been shown in many clinical trials to lower LDL, the “bad” cholesterin, and reduce the risk of heart attacks and strokes. The most widely used statins are atorvastatin (Lipitor), simvastatin (Zocor), rosuvastatin (Crestor), and pravastatin (Cholestral). Lowering LDL by just 1% can reduce the risk of heart disease by 1 to 2%."},{"id":"T2","text":"Hyperlipidemia involves elevated levels of lipids like cholesterol and triglycerides in the bloodstream, which is a significant risk factor for conditions such as coronary heart disease, including heart attacks and coronary artery disease. Various medications exist to manage these lipid levels and mitigate the risks.

