Each of these figures brings both assistance and hindrance to Odysseus. Athena’s wisdom and Poseidon’s warnings are invaluable, yet they come with strict conditions that Odysseus must adhere to. Polyphemus’s help, while unexpected, is essential for survival. Despite their differing natures, all three characters challenge Odysseus to choose between following divine directives and safeguarding his own life—a dilemma that resonates with many modern individuals."},{"id":"T4","text":"Athena, Poseidon and Polyphemus are the three principal characters in Homer’s Odyssey. They appear at different points in the story and their roles change from one scene to another. Athena, the goddess of wisdom, gives Odysseus a fleet of ships and guides him on his journey. Poseidon, the god of the sea, gives him the power to navigate through unknown seas. Polyphemus, the all-seeing monster, helps Odysseus by warning the Cyclops that a storm is coming. Athena, Poseidon and Polyphemus all bring benefits to Odysseus, but they are also obstacles to his journey, because they all demand that he follow their strict rules and not deviate from the path they have set for him."}]}

