One time, I asked her about her secret ingredient, but she only smiled mysteriously and said, "It's a secret." I didn't press further, but as I grew older, I noticed that my mother was growing increasingly protective of the recipe. She guarded it zealously, treating it almost like a prized possession, ensuring no one ever got ahold of it."},{"id":"T4","text":"I never liked my mother's cookies. They were too sweet for me, and they never came out of the oven properly. But the whole town disagreed with me, and every year my mother, who was known as the "cookie lady" in the neighborhood, baked hundreds of batches of cookies during the Christmas season, and people came from far and wide to taste them. I once asked her what was in them, but she only smiled and said, "It's a secret." I didn't ask her again, but as I grew older, I noticed that my mother was becoming more and more jealous of the recipe, and she was guarding it as if it were a precious jewel, and never let it slip from her hands."}]}

{"genre":"Reuters","passages":[{"id":"T1","text":"In August 1997, U.S. Vice President Al Gore made a trip to mainland China, where he met with Chinese Vice Premier Li Peng. This visit came after criticism and protests from China’s communist government regarding the release of the U.S. documentary "China's Rain of Torture." 

Gore's visit marked progress in U.S.-Chinese relations. Following the 1989 trip by his predecessor, Dan Quayle, China had become increasingly critical of certain U.S. officials. The latest instance was the release of "China's Rain of Torture," which highlighted the Chinese government's use of torture to quell the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests and subsequent crackdown."},{"id":"T2","text":"The American Vice-President Al Gore visited China in August 1997, and met with Chinese Vice-President Li Peng. According to the Washington Times and other media reports, the vice-president took this step in response to the Chinese Communist Party's protests against the release of the American film "China's Tortured Rain" in China last month. This visit is a breakthrough in U.S.-China relations. Since the visit of his predecessor, former U.S. Vice-President Dan Quayle, the Chinese Communist Party has been resolutely opposing some American leaders. The most recent case is the release of the American film "China's Tortured Rain" last month. This film is about the Chinese Communist Party's use of torture to suppress the 1989 Tiananmen Massacre."},{"id":"T3","text":"The American vice-president Al Gore visited China in August 1997, and met with Chinese vice-premier Li Peng. According to the Washington Times and other media reports, the vice-president took this step in response to the Chinese communist regime's protests and criticisms of the American documentary "China's Tortured Rain." This visit was a breakthrough in U.S.-China relations. Since the former U.S. vice-president Dan Quayle's visit to the Chinese communist regime in 1989, the Chinese communist regime has been taking a hard line against some U.S. leaders. The most recent case was the release of the American documentary "China's Tortured Rain," which depicts the Chinese communist regime's use of torture to suppress the 1989 Tiananmen Massacre."},{"id":"T4","text":"In August 1997, U.S. Vice President Al Gore made a trip to the mainland of China, during which he met with Chinese Vice Premier Li Peng. According to reports by The Washington Times and other media outlets, Gore’s visit was a response to the Chinese communist regime’s criticism and protests regarding the release of the U.S. documentary "China's Rain of Torture" in China earlier that month.

This trip marked a significant improvement in U.S.-Chinese relations. Since Vice President Dan Quayle’s visit to the Chinese communist regime in 1989, there had been a hard stance from the regime against some U.S. leaders. The latest example was the controversial release of the documentary "China's Rain of Torture," which highlighted the regime’s use of torture during the Tiananmen Square protests and subsequent crackdown in 1989."}]}

